This is a classic DP problem. Let dp[i] be the number of distinct subsequences of S[:i]. For each character, we can either include it or exclude it. If we include it, we add dp[i-1] to dp[i]. If we exclude it, we add dp[i-1] to dp[i]. But we must also consider the case where we include the previous character, since it might be part of a longer subsequence. So we add dp[i-2] to dp[i]. We also need to consider the case where we include the previous character and the current character, since they might be part of a longer subsequence. So we add dp[i-2] to dp[i]. We also need to consider the case where we include the previous character and the current character and the previous character, since they might be part of a longer subsequence. So we add dp[i-3] to dp[i]. We also need to consider the case where we include the previous character and the current character and the previous character and the previous character, since they might be part of a longer subsequence. So we add dp[i-4] to dp[i]. And so on. We can see that this is a DP problem, and we can solve it in O(n^2) time with O(n) space.
</reasoning>

<solution>
```python
def distinct_subsequences(S):
    n = len(S)
    dp = [0] * (n + 1)
    dp[0] = 1
